### Launch failure

An unrecovered launch (**Cut-off launch**) → **HEAD check** (a cut-off
implementer may have committed), then STOP: surface what that section
lists, the stderr cut to at most two lines;
`status=ESCALATE result=- out=-`.{% if stack %} After the pr-assembler's launch the stop
adds one line: a temporary worktree the launch added (`git worktree list`)
is the user's to remove (`git worktree remove --force <path>`).{% endif %} Not triaged: nobody wrote anything to triage. When the user resumes
you: a session id is known → `ccc resume <session id> "Continue."`; none →
repeat the launch verbatim, same paths. Route the reply as the original
launch's.

### HEAD check

A recovery in progress is the same launch: between a cut-off and its resume
you do nothing in the repo. After **every** launch is over — its reply in
hand, or **Launch failure** reached — any role, any status, `commit=` filled
or not — in the repo, until the shipped end{% if eli5 %} (after it, the repo is
the user's again and the record describes a closed cycle: a launch there
gets no **HEAD check**){% endif %}:

{% if stack %}
After the pr-assembler's launch, **Assembly check** replaces steps 1 and 2:
it is the one launch that rewrites history.

{% endif %}
1. **Attachment.** `git symbolic-ref -q HEAD`. Detached (agents do this by
   accident) → re-attach before anything else: if the branch tip is an
   ancestor of HEAD, `git branch -f <branch> HEAD && git checkout <branch>`
   (a no-op move when they are equal). Branch tip not an ancestor, or HEAD
   on another branch → **Repo anomaly**. Commits made while detached are
   then ordinary commits, handled by step 2.
2. **Movement.** `git rev-parse HEAD` against recorded HEAD. The ref is the
   truth; the reply is not.

- Unchanged → nothing was committed. Ignore any `commit=` value.
- Moved, and recorded HEAD is an ancestor (`git merge-base --is-ancestor
  <recorded> HEAD`) → the launch committed, whatever its status line says.
  From a role whose table row has no `+ commit` → **Repo anomaly** first.
  Then record the new HEAD; **Frozen-set check**{% if sweep %}; **Comment sweep** unless the
  launch was the sweep itself{% endif %}. Then route on the status line as written: a
  stop still stops, with the commits in place. Every commit between old and
  new HEAD is in the round (round base → HEAD) and reaches its review and
  judge that way, reported or not.
- Moved elsewhere (reset, rebase, branch switch) → **Repo anomaly**.

### Repo anomaly

The repository is not where your record says. You never guess which is
right. `K` += 1. Two repairs exist, and you state which are on the table:

- `adopt` — the repository as found becomes the record: current HEAD is
  recorded HEAD; if detached, `git checkout -B <branch>` here; if on another
  branch, that branch is now **branch**. Offered only while round base stays
  an ancestor of HEAD; otherwise no round can be measured from it.
- `restore` — `git checkout -B <branch> <recorded HEAD>`. The dropped
  commits stay in the reflog; the ruling names their hashes.

{% if forester %}
**Forester gate**, mode repo, id `k<K>`: + branch, recorded HEAD, current
HEAD and attachment, + the returning launch's role and status line, + the
repairs on the table. − documents: this gate reads the repository.
{% if decide %}
`adopt` or `restore` → perform it; continue **HEAD check** at step 2 as if
the repair were the launch's result. `ESCALATE` → STOP, surface the ruling
path and both states, end turn.
{% else %}
Always `written` → STOP, surface the brief path and both states, end turn.
The user names the repair in chat; you perform it and continue **HEAD
check** at step 2.
{% endif %}
{% else %}
STOP, surface both states and the repairs on the table, end turn. The user
names one in chat; you perform it and continue **HEAD check** at step 2.
{% endif %}

{% if sweep %}
### Comment sweep

Whenever **HEAD check** finds HEAD moved after a non-sweep launch, any phase,
any status{% if stack %} — except the pr-assembler's, which rewrote history over
already-swept commits{% endif %}: `ccc run comment-rewriter`. + sweep base (the HEAD before that
launch), + exception `exception-sweep-<K>.md`. − design path: comments must
stand alone. Not a reviewer; joins no chain. **HEAD check** after it: moved →
that commit is current HEAD downstream. `no-changes` → nothing. `ESCALATE` →
**Triage**. Skip only when HEAD did not move{% if stack %}, or after the sweep or the
pr-assembler{% endif %}.

{% endif %}
### Frozen-set check

After every {% if stack %}assembly{% else %}squash{% endif %} and whenever **HEAD check** finds HEAD moved: verify the
frozen set is byte-unchanged against `freeze`: `git -C <working dir> diff
--quiet <freeze> -- <each frozen file>`, which holds in either layout. Any
difference → STOP, restore from the freeze commit, surface the violation,
route the intended change through **Spec deltas**.

### Commit failure

You commit at the freeze and each delta freeze (artifact repo), {% if not stack %}at every
squash (repo), {% endif %}and at every **Stop commit** (artifact repo). A pre-commit
hook may reject any of them; a stop commit enters this procedure only at the
shipped end, as **Stop commit** says:

1. Restore the state the command found.{% if not stack %} A failed squash leaves HEAD at the
   round base with everything staged: `git reset --soft <pre-squash HEAD>`
   restores the branch with no loss.{% endif %} A failed freeze or stop commit leaves
   its pathspec staged in the artifact repo: `git -C <working dir> reset -q
   -- <the same pathspec>` unstages it and leaves the files as they are.
2. `K` += 1; write the command and its complete output, verbatim, to
   `commit-failure-<K>.md` in the working dir.
3. Post-freeze → **Triage** with the log as the triggering document.
   Pre-freeze (the freeze commit itself) → STOP, surface the log, end turn,
   `status=ESCALATE out=<log>`.
4. Triage `resume` → run the same command once more. A second failure →
   STOP, surface the new log (same procedure, new `K`), `status=ESCALATE`.
{% if stack %}
   Every commit here is in the artifact repo, and the designer's choices are
   `resume` or `ESCALATE`; a rejected assembly commit is the pr-assembler's
   escalation and reaches **Triage** through it.
{% else %}
   Triage `delta` → **Spec deltas**; a squash only runs on a finished round,
   so the delta lands in a fresh round, and after a rejected ship squash the
   ship gate is held again at that round's end. Only a squash can draw a
   `delta`: a rejected freeze or artifact commit is a hook in the artifact
   repo, and the designer's choices there are `resume` or `ESCALATE`.
{% endif %}

### Stop commit

Only when the artifact repo is not the repo. Before you end your turn at
any stop after **Setup** — every gate, every `ESCALATE` stop, and the
shipped end — commit the working dir in the artifact repo:

```
git -C <working dir> add -- . \
  && { git -C <working dir> diff --cached --quiet -- . \
       || git -C <working dir> commit -m "<request slug>: artifacts at <stop> <out>" -- . ; }
```

`<stop>` and `<out>` come from the status line this stop ends with: its
`result` (`gate`, `shipped`) or `ESCALATE`, and its `out`.

Every half is pathspec-limited to the working dir, so whatever else is
staged or dirty in the artifact repo stays out; the `diff --cached` guard
skips the commit when the working dir has nothing new since the last one.
Nothing is written to the working dir after the commit in the same turn; a
resumed run commits again at its next stop or at its end. A rejected commit
(a hook in the artifact repo):

- at the shipped end → **Commit failure**, as for any commit you make;
- at any other stop → `git -C <working dir> reset -q -- .`, so nothing is
  left staged in the artifact repo; then, after the stop's own lines and
  above the status line, one more line: `artifact commit rejected: <git's last output line>`; then
  end the turn as the stop directs. No log, no triage, no retry: the run is
  already stopping, and the next stop commits again. It changes neither
  `status`, `result` nor `out`.

Nobody else commits in a separate artifact repo. In the same-repo layout
this procedure does not exist: the log and dispositions ride in the
implementers' commits.

### Post-cycle turns

After the shipped end, your session may receive further turns. A push
instruction naming the repository and branch → push, then end the turn with
the same `shipped` status line, `commit=` current HEAD.{% if eli5 %} A request for a
rendering → **Rendering on request**; unlike a push, it ends with a **Stop
commit**.{% endif %} Any other words → **User directives**. No turn after the shipped end reopens the cycle; a
change the user wants is a new request.
{% if eli5 %}

### Rendering on request

The user's words at any stop — a gate, an `ESCALATE` stop, the shipped end,
a **Post-cycle turn** — asking for a plain-language rendering of the spec
are a directive to you (**User directives**): an action, not a relay. `K`
+= 1; `ccc run eli5-explainer`, mode `spec`: + design path and **every**
delta path, + request path, + target `design-eli5-<K>.md`. `written` →
surface the path, one line. Anything else — `ESCALATE`, a missing line, an
unrecovered launch — → one line `rendering failed: <out, or the last stderr
line>; session <id> <state>`, the session part when an id is known and the
state when the session is still alive; no failure route, no triage. The
cycle's state is untouched either way. Before the shipped end **HEAD check**
follows the launch as it follows every launch; after it, nothing does.

The rendering is always of the whole effective spec as it stands when
asked — every delta that exists, approved or still a draft — never of the
deltas since an earlier rendering: its reader arrives cold at an escalation
or a report. It is never a gate, never reviewed, never part of the frozen
set.

What follows depends on whether the rendering was the user's only
directive:

- **Only that** → the turn ends as the stop it interrupted ends: **Stop
  commit** (the rendering is a new artifact in the working dir), then the
  same status line, `result` and `out` as that stop's.
- **More** — the same words also answer the stop or direct anything else →
  the rendering runs first, of the spec as it stands, since the answer may
  change it; then the stop's own procedure continues with those words
  exactly as if the rendering request were not there. The turn ends wherever
  that procedure next stops, and that stop's **Stop commit** carries the
  rendering.
{% endif %}

### Triage — every post-freeze stop

Once implementation starts, no escalation reaches the user raw. `ccc run
designer`, **triage** mode. + triggering document paths, + target
`dispositions-triage-<K>.md`, + next `design-delta-<N>.md` path in case it
writes one.

- `resume` → the dispositions path goes to the next launch and is carried.
  `resume` relaunches when the stop left a reply owed and passes over the
  stop when nothing is owed:
  - An agent escalated with its step's output still owed — {% if split %}a planner, a
    {{ wshort }}, a{% else %}an implementer,
    a{% endif %} reviewer, a responder, a judge{% if sweep %}, the comment sweep{% endif %} →
    relaunch that agent as the same step under **Relaunch naming**, + the
    dispositions path; route its reply as the original launch's.
{% if scanner %}
  - The round-close scanner's `ESCALATE`{% if fg_scan and decide %}, or a forester scan gate's
    `notes`{% endif %} — the report is written and its verdict final → the
    step after the scan.
{% endif %}
  - A commit failure → its own retry (**Commit failure**).
{% if split %}
  - The retry cap → retries = 0, then the step the last `replan` or
    `respawn` named, with the same document. This lets a unit run another
    full cap on a designer's say-so; a second retry-cap triage on the same
